{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## The trigger, identified: this environment forbids interactive git credentials, so every rebar auto-push fails the same way\n\nThe sentinel this ticket is about is created by a push failure. That push failure is not\nintermittent and not a network fault — it is structural in this environment, and it will\nrecreate the sentinel indefinitely until either side is addressed.\n\n### What blocks the push\n\nThe harness injects git configuration through `GIT_CONFIG_COUNT`/`GIT_CONFIG_KEY_*` rather\nthan through any config file. Among the 30 injected pairs:\n\n```\n1: credential.interactive = never\n5: credential.helper       = (empty — the configured helpers are cleared)\n```\n\nand the process environment additionally carries `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0`.\n\nTogether those three make it impossible for git to obtain a credential by any interactive or\nhelper-mediated route. The observable symptom is exactly the string recorded in the sentinel's\n`detail` field:\n\n```\nfatal: unable to get password from user\n```\n\nThat is the same message captured in the stale marker examined earlier on this ticket, which\nconfirms the marker was produced by this mechanism rather than by a transport problem.\n\n### Why the usual escapes do not work\n\nMeasured, each independently:\n\n- **`GIT_ASKPASS` is ignored.** A working askpass script — verified standalone, returning the\n  correct 42-character secret — is never consulted, because `credential.interactive = never`\n  short-circuits credential acquisition before askpass is reached.\n- **The OS keychain is empty for this host, and cannot be populated.** Probing\n  `git credential-osxkeychain get` returns nothing, and a `git credential approve` that should\n  store the secret completes without error yet leaves the subsequent probe still empty. The\n  write does not take effect, so \"just store it in the keychain once\" is not available here.\n- **Clearing `credential.helper` alone does not help**, because the interactive prohibition is\n  a separate key.\n\nThe combination that does work is overriding the injected key for a single invocation:\n\n```\nenv GIT_ASKPASS=<script> GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=1 \\\n  git -c credential.interactive=always -c credential.helper= push <remote> <refspec>\n```\n\n### Why this matters to this ticket specifically\n\n`rebar`'s internal push scrubs `GIT_CONFIG_*` from the environment it hands to git. That scrub\nremoves the injected `credential.interactive = never` — but it also removes everything else,\nand rebar does not supply an askpass or any other credential source of its own. So rebar's\npush has no route to a credential either, fails, and records the sentinel. A human can then\ndeliver the events by hand with the override above, which leaves the store level with its\nremote and the sentinel permanently asserting a backlog that no longer exists.\n\nThat is the complete loop this ticket documents: a structurally guaranteed push failure feeds\na sentinel that nothing ever falsifies. Fixing the freshness check makes the gate honest, but\nthe pushes will keep failing until the credential path is addressed separately. The two are\nworth tracking as distinct defects — this ticket should stay scoped to the sentinel never\nbeing reconciled against git, since that is the part that wrongly blocks a healthy gate.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789718414558552618,"uuid":"820c6e53-b644-4f51-8218-c9abc6eaa15a"}